    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've tried the fresh file approach and cleared the cache, but it didn't help. It's almost as if the errors are intrinsic to the text itself. I find myself staring at the footnotes, and they just feel so... uninvited. There's this sense of small, busy movements in the margins, like these tiny, frantic things are scurrying around the names and dates, rearranging them just enough to be wrong but not enough to be immediately obvious. It's incredibly draining. Does this kind of \"interference\" from small, repetitive, and seemingly sentient errors happen often in high-level academic publishing, or am i just losing my grip on reality?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the feeling of being watched by microscopic textual saboteurs is certainly exhausting, you are not alone in dealing with maddening software glitches, though perhaps not with sentient footnotes. These persistent, subtle errors often arise when citation managers interact poorly with complex document features like tracked changes, nested tables, or obscure style guides. It's often a deeply buried conflict between the software's logic and the document's structure, which can feel suspiciously like a mischievous, unseen force.\n\nTo try and pin down this \"interference,\" I suggest isolating the problem by testing a small section—perhaps just one page with a few citations—and running a complete, clean conversion of that snippet into a plain text file, and then back into your word processor. This forces the software to re-evaluate every single character without the baggage of the entire monograph. If the errors persist even in that tiny sample, then the issue is likely rooted in the citation data itself, not the document's environment.",
      "source": "target",
